  "narrative": "Combo F (Recovery) dominates at week 10 of its MEDIUM duration episode, driven by CAPE at 42.7 (99th percentile), signaling extreme valuations amid a late-cycle Fed cutting environment. This 79% 6m hit rate signal outweighs competing bearish signals due to its proven medium-term reliability and direct alignment with current Fed easing dynamics.\n\nCombo F (Recovery) remains ACTIVE since 2026-04-03 with a bullish SPX bias, delivering a 79% 6m hit rate with +5.5% average returns as CAPE extreme valuations coincide with late-cycle Fed cuts. Combo E (Valuation Extreme) sits CONFIRMED with CAPE and NFCI legs active, showing a 19% 12m hit rate but +10.9% average returns when successful, as CAPE at 99th percentile and NFCI at -0.494 create conflicting valuation-liquidity dynamics. Combo B (Capitulation) holds on WATCH status with no active signals as VIX at 18.92 and CFTC positioning at -503,509 (1st percentile) suggest oversold conditions but lack the volatility spike required for activation.\n\nThe Recovery signal wins on three fronts: (1) CAPE at 42.7 represents peak valuation stress that historically resolves through Fed accommodation rather than crash scenarios during cutting cycles, (2) NFCI at -0.494 shows financial conditions remain accommodative despite extreme valuations, supporting the Fed's ability to engineer soft landing outcomes, and (3) WTI at -12.0% (2nd percentile) creates disinflationary pressure that extends Fed cutting runway beyond current market pricing. The CUTTING_LATE regime with NORMAL curve and EXTREME valuations mirrors 1999-2000 and 2007 precedents where Fed pivot timing proved critical for intermediate-term equity performance.\n\nThe closest analog remains 1999 Q4 when CAPE reached 44.2 amid Fed easing, generating +8.2% 3m, +12.1% 6m, and -15.4% 12m returns before the dot-com correction. The 2007 Q3 episode with CAPE at 27.3 during initial Fed cuts produced +3.1% 3m, -8.7% 6m, and -38.2% 12m as credit conditions deteriorated. Current conditions most closely match 1999 given the absence of credit stress (HY spreads at 2.76% vs 5.8% in 2007), suggesting the intermediate-term bullish case remains intact while long-term risks accumulate.\n\nSystem posture remains TACTICAL EASY MONEY based on Recovery dominance and accommodative financial conditions. Posture shifts to TACTICAL FEARFUL on: (1) VIX sustained above 25 for 5d triggering Combo B activation, (2) NFCI rising above +0.5 for 10d indicating tightening financial conditions, or (3) Fed pause signals emerging before CAPE retreats below 35. Upcoming CPI release provides the next catalyst for regime confirmation or challenge.",
